<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 10th Grade (CEFR: B1.1)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> Dream Jobs</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> What Skills Do I Need for a Future Career?</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Communication: Assist in negotiations between student groups. Text: Mediate information from notices or announcements for broader understanding.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can assist groups negotiating job ideas by simplifying phrases (e.g., "Can we pick one dream job to research?" while showing job options like "doctor, teacher, artist" on a chart). Can mediate information from job announcements with visuals (e.g., pointing to job descriptions like "Skills needed: computer skills" in a flyer). Can explain terms from job notices, using examples (e.g., "Requires teamwork" by saying, "You work with others," and pointing to a teamwork image).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a short, slightly complex job description for a "Future Innovation Strategist" (e.g., "Requires robust adaptability and foresight in emerging technological fields"). Students work in pairs to identify words or phrases they think a younger student or a non-expert might find difficult to understand. The teacher asks: "Which part of this job description might need simplifying?" and "How would you explain 'robust adaptability' simply?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to simplify a complex phrase from the job description, emphasizing clarity and using simpler vocabulary. For example, "Foresight in emerging technological fields" can be mediated as "You need to be able to predict new developments in technology" or "You will anticipate how technology will evolve." The teacher explicitly uses the word "mediate" and "simplify."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I mediate information, am I making it more complex or easier to understand?" (Expected: Easier to understand.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When would you need to mediate a job announcement for a friend?" (Expected: If it has difficult words or requires explanation.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Dream Jobs")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents an authentic-looking "Future Jobs Bulletin" or a digital job announcement for a new role like "Remote Work Facilitator" or "AI Ethics Consultant." This notice will include sections like "Key Responsibilities," "Required Skills," and "Future Outlook," using vocabulary such as `automation`, `advancement`, `adaptability`, `employment`, `artificial intelligence`, `remote work`, `evolve`, `require`, `demand`, `innovation`, `workforce`. It will also contain sentences using future simple ("Many new jobs will emerge"), future continuous ("In ten years, people will be working..."), and modals for possibility ("Jobs may require..."). Visuals are incorporated, similar to a flyer. </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Mediate information from job announcements or notices for broader understanding.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Questions:</b> "What is the main purpose of this job announcement?" "What kind of skills does this future job mostly demand?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Identification:</b> Students identify three specific skills mentioned and underline any vocabulary they think might be unclear to a peer. (e.g., `digital fluency`, `cross-functional collaboration`, `proactive problem-solving`).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Prediction:</b> "What will people be doing in this field in the next five years, according to the 'Future Outlook' section?" (Targets future continuous).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Mediation)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice mediating complex job-related information using future simple, future continuous, and modals for possibilities, alongside target vocabulary related to future jobs and skills (e.g., `automation`, `advancement`, `adaptability`, `employment`, `artificial intelligence`, `remote work`, `evolve`, `require`, `demand`, `innovation`, `workforce`). They will also focus on the pronunciation of /dʒ/ in "change" and /ʒ/ in "vision" and stress in multisyllabic words like "automation," "advancement," and "adaptability."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: "Simplify It!" Matching:</b> The teacher provides a worksheet with two columns. Column A lists complex phrases from various job notices or career predictions (e.g., "Implement agile methodologies," "Foster a culture of continuous learning," "Requires robust critical thinking capabilities"). Column B provides simplified explanations. Students match the complex phrases with their mediated counterparts. (e.g., "Implement agile methodologies" -> "Use flexible work methods"). The teacher encourages students to pronounce words like "advancement" and "adaptability" clearly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Substitution Drill: "Future Job Translator":</b> In small groups, students receive cards with job-related statements that include grammar targets. One student reads a statement (e.g., "Many companies will demand a high degree of digital fluency"). Another student mediates it using a different structure or simpler vocabulary, incorporating modals or future continuous if appropriate (e.g., "Companies will likely require people who are good with computers," or "People may need strong computer skills"). The teacher circulates, monitoring for correct use of `will` / `will be V-ing` / `may` / `might`, and checking pronunciation, especially for /dʒ/ and /ʒ/.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction: "Career Idea Negotiators":</b> Students work in pairs. Each student receives a "dream job" card with 2-3 specific, somewhat complex skill requirements or future trends related to that job (e.g., "Data Scientist: Must analyze Big Data for predictive `automation`," or "Environmental Engineer: Will `develop` sustainable `innovations` to `mitigate` climate `change` /dʒ/"). They then take turns "pitching" their dream job to an imaginary panel (represented by the other student) and mediating the complex terms or predictions, explaining them in a way that assists in "negotiating" the best understanding of the job idea. They should use phrases like, "What this means is..." or "To put it simply..." The teacher reminds them to consider their audience (peer vs. mentor) for tone and formality.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">15-20 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Dream Jobs")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Group Presentation of Mediated Interview Segments.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b> Students work in their "Career Interview Video" project groups. Their task is to select 2-3 questions or potential answers from their prepared interview script (from previous lessons) that contain complex terms, predictions about `future` `employment`, or descriptions of `technological` `advancement` related to their chosen professional's field. </p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group will present a short segment (2-3 minutes) demonstrating how they will mediate this information for their video's audience.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">One student might act as the interviewer, asking a question like, "How do you foresee the `workforce's` `adaptability` evolving with increased `automation`?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Another student acts as the "mediator," stepping in to simplify or rephrase the question or the *potential answer* from the professional for the video audience, saying, "What [Interviewer's Name] is asking is, 'How flexible will workers need to be when more jobs are automated?'" or after a hypothetical complex answer, "To clarify, the professional means that jobs `may require` new `skills` like creative problem-solving."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Groups must use visuals (e.g., showing a chart of job options, pointing to an image representing a skill like 'computer skills' or 'teamwork') as they mediate, aligning with the learning outcome. They should incorporate `future simple` for predictions, `future continuous` for ongoing trends, and `modals` for possibilities (`may`, `might`, `will`). They will explicitly mention the `skills` needed for `evolving` job `markets`.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students will present their mediated segment of the "Career Interview Video" script to the class. The teacher will observe using a checklist, focusing on their mediation skills.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Clear articulation of words, especially contrastive sounds (/dʒ/, /ʒ/) and stress in multisyllabic words (`automation`, `advancement`, `adaptability`).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> Appropriate integration of terms like `automation`, `advancement`, `adaptability`, `innovation`, `workforce` in both complex and simplified explanations.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and Accuracy of Mediation:</b> Effectively simplifying complex phrases or ideas without losing essential meaning; using `future simple`, `future continuous`, and `modals` correctly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Active participation and effective teamwork within the group to achieve the mediation goal.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Effective Use of Visuals/Examples:</b> Using visuals or real-life examples to aid in clarification.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most challenging part of simplifying complex job information for your project?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did our group ensure everyone understood the mediated information before presenting?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Reflecting on today's mediation tasks, how has your ability to simplify and explain complex ideas about future `jobs` and `skills` improved?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new `adaptability` skills do you think *you* `will` need to `acquire` for your `future` `career`, and how `will` you `prepare` for them?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well during the mediation activities? Students demonstrated strong capacity to simplify `technological` terms and `future` `predictions`. The use of visuals significantly aided understanding.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed? Some students still struggle with adapting their tone and formality for different audiences (e.g., explaining `automation` to a younger sibling vs. a peer). More explicit modeling of sociolinguistic strategies might be beneficial.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence: This being the final lesson (5 of 5) for the "Dream Jobs" theme, the next steps will involve the actual recording and production of the "Career Interview Video" projects, where students will apply all `communicative` `competences` developed throughout the unit, with a strong focus on mediation for clarity.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>